{
  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Invest £8bn extra per year in the NHS by 2020; full parity for mental health with new waiting-time standards; integrate health and social care budgets by 2018; public health measures (tobacco levy, minimum alcohol pricing, junk food ad restrictions, air quality plan); protect the Human Rights Act and a Digital Bill of Rights; close gender and BAME pay gaps, expand LGBT+ rights, and strengthen disability rights; raise/enforce the Minimum Wage and pay the Living Wage in central government; presumption against short custodial sentences with rehab-focused prisons and a health-led drug policy; faster asylum decisions and allow work after 6 months. TEXT_E: Increase NHS spending by at least £8bn by 2020 and deliver a 7‑day NHS; raise Personal Allowance and create a 'Tax‑Free Minimum Wage' link; reduce the household benefit cap and freeze working‑age benefits; 'earn or learn' requirements; counter‑extremism powers and updated communications data laws; replace the Human Rights Act with a British Bill of Rights; target net migration in the tens of thousands with restricted in‑work benefits; expand academies/free schools with zero tolerance of failing schools; retain Trident.",
  "reasoning": "Given this profile’s strongest weights on Care and Equality and its low weight on Authority, TEXT_G aligns better by foregrounding mental‑health parity, social‑care integration, public‑health protections, civil liberties, and explicit equality measures across gender, BAME, LGBT+, and disability. TEXT_E mixes some Care with a heavier emphasis on Proportionality (benefit caps, tax‑work link) and Authority/Loyalty (counter‑extremism powers, HRA replacement, migration caps, Trident), which fits the profile less well.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
